seg-ceea5588c1fd = A mapping file (<c1/>, TOML) tells Kshana how to read an export it has no reader for, and turns it into a <a1><c2/></a1> observation file. No code is written: the file names the columns, their units and the time format.

# text
seg-40c0b4644f59 = Step by step, for a monitoring station: <a1>guides/MONITORING-STATION.md</a1>. Step by step, for scoring an interference classifier on the benchmark: <a2>guides/BENCHMARK-CLASSES.md</a2>.

# text
# c1: examples/connect/
# c2: tests/connect_examples.rs
seg-23d577072e9f = Three worked examples are in <a1><c1/></a1>: an Android raw measurement CSV (one row per satellite, satellites from a constellation code and a number), a timing receiver's semicolon-separated monitoring export (one column per satellite, GPS week and time of week, height in feet) and a monitoring service's nested JSON (position in radians). <c2/> maps all three.

# text
seg-21590aa79508 = The file

# text
# c1: scale
# c2: offset
# c3: value × scale + offset
# c4: column
# c5: /
seg-d4aab3d5ba8b = Every field takes <c1/> and <c2/> (applied after the unit: <c3/>), and in JSON a <c4/> starting with <c5/> is a JSON pointer into nested objects.

