# c2: kshana.obs/1
seg-76731afb3d12 = <c1/> follows the files a logger writes or rotates into a directory and returns the <c2/> records that became complete since the last step, through the same mapping file.

# text
# c1: t_s
seg-34a939685c17 = The files whose names end with the chosen suffix are read in name order, as one continuous log: a later file's comment lines and header row are dropped. So an epoch whose rows a rotation split is still one epoch, and every <c1/> counts from the directory's first epoch.

# text
seg-c6ff0149b72a = Only complete lines are read; a line still being written waits for the next step.

# text
# c1: flush
seg-67b34d98a802 = The last epoch is held back until rows after it arrive, because more rows of it may still be written. <c1/> releases it at the end of a run.

# text
# c1: jsonl
seg-5372c7cca6a3 = Files must be in time order by name. A single JSON document is refused (use <c1/>).

# text
seg-c67774293144 = It polls, with no operating-system file events, so it behaves the same on every platform and on network file systems. The directory is re-read on each step, so move processed files away from time to time.

# text
# c1: kshana connect map
# c2: tests/connect_watch.rs
seg-cdcecfea5bd7 = Checked by replaying a log written in pieces and rotated part-way through an epoch: the result is the same as <c1/> on the whole log (<c2/>).

