seg-bdeb73927fcd = Live MQTT subscription (<c1/>)

# text
# c1: kshana::connect_mqtt
# c2: kshana.obs/1
seg-ef85d6872444 = <c1/> subscribes to a topic on an MQTT broker and maps the rows that arrive, with the same mapping file, into <c2/> records.

# text
# c1: +
# c2: #
seg-62fdcb3537e5 = MQTT { "3.1.1" } over TCP: clean session, optional user name and password, one topic filter (<c1/> and <c2/> work), QoS { "0" } or { "1" }. A QoS { "1" } message is acknowledged. A ping is sent when the line has been quiet for half the keep-alive.

# text
# c1: jsonl
# c2: csv
# c3: tsv
# c4: connect watch
# c5: --header
seg-38b8166835cb = Each message is one or more rows of the mapping's format (<c1/>, <c2/> or <c3/>). The messages are read in arrival order as one continuous log, as <c4/> reads a directory, and the last epoch is held back until a later row arrives. Publishers send rows without a header, so a CSV or TSV mapping needs the header row given (<c5/>).

# text
# c1: t_s
seg-62c2734f5ae6 = Rows already emitted are dropped as soon as their epoch is emitted, and <c1/> still counts from the first epoch of the run. Each message therefore costs the same however long the subscription has run, and memory stays bounded.

# text
seg-7130a8330abc = TLS is not built in, and the default build adds no crate for MQTT. For a broker that only accepts TLS, run a local TLS relay (for example stunnel, or a local broker bridged to it) and subscribe to that.

# text
# c1: kshana connect map
# c2: tests/connect_mqtt.rs
seg-f054551b6c7e = Checked against a loopback broker that publishes a log one row per message, alternating QoS { "0" } and { "1" }: the result is the same as <c1/> on the whole log, with the trimming path exercised (<c2/>).

